# Recorded by hand from a reference runtime; see docs/reference-runtime.md.
# The model this refers to is NOT committed -- see docs/fixture-provenance.md
# for where to obtain it. No mandatory test reads this file.
#
# This one is here because the whole Gemma family answered in fluent
# nonsense for a month and nothing in this repository could tell. The engine,
# the fixtures and Reference_Transformer all held that a Gemma normalization
# gain is one plus the stored weight, as the architecture states it -- and
# the converter that writes a Gemma GGUF has already added that one to every
# norm weight, so all three normalized to two plus the weight and agreed
# with each other through every crossing. What tells them apart from the
# published runtime is the file, which this recording asks about.
#
# Tokenization came from:
#   llama-tokenize -m gemma-3-1b-it-Q4_K_M.gguf
#     -p "The capital of France is"
#     -> [2, 818, 5279, 529, 7001, 563]
#   where 2 is the beginning marker this vocabulary asks for.
#
# Greedy continuation came from llama-completion with --temp 0
# --no-conversation, which printed " Paris." for the same prompt.
#
# The logits are the first three values of result_output from
# llama-eval-callback at the position after the prompt. The tolerance is 0.3
# rather than the 0.2 of the other four-bit recording, and the reason is
# worth knowing: this engine puts the third at -0.9814, and a float32 pass
# over the exactly dequantized weights, written independently in numpy for
# the occasion, puts it at -0.9814 as well -- it is the reference runtime
# that sits 0.27 away, because it quantizes its activations to eight bits
# before each product and this engine does not. Twenty-six layers and a
# 262,144-row output projection widen what that costs. The first two agree
# to within 0.07.

runtime llama.cpp b10595-95b8e33e1
model gemma-3-1b-it-Q4_K_M.gguf
prompt The capital of France is
tokens 2 818 5279 529 7001 563
greedy_text \sParis.
logit 0 -11.2844
logit 1 -1.4279
logit 2 -0.7139
tolerance 0.3
